//? Title: Multi-Goal Best-First Search
//? Subtitle: Lower bounds, queue removals and reopening with datamunge's b_star_search
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Given a directed graph with nonnegative edge costs, find the cheapest route from
one start to any vertex in a goal set. The first goal discovered need not be the
cheapest. We derive the lower-bound stopping rule, inspect a four-vertex example
by hand, and show why inconsistent estimates require reopening vertices.
The historical `b_star_search` API implements multi-goal A*: its result is optimal
when the supplied estimate never exceeds the distance to the nearest goal and
is zero at every goal. An overestimating example demonstrates the limit.
)

\toc

> The method

Let \(T\) be a nonempty goal set. For each vertex retain the best discovered
cost \(g(v)\) from the start and a parent pointer. Queue vertices by

$$
f(v)=g(v)+h(v),\qquad h(v)\le d(v,T)=\min_{t\in T}d(v,t),\qquad h(t)=0\quad(t\in T).
$$
\alttext(The queue key is discovered path cost plus a lower bound to the nearest goal. Every goal has estimate zero.)

Remove the smallest key, ignoring obsolete entries. If the vertex is a goal,
return its parent path. Otherwise relax every outgoing edge: a strictly smaller
cost replaces the stored label and queues another entry, even if this vertex
was expanded earlier. Equal priorities prefer the smaller vertex identifier;
equal-cost alternatives retain their original parent.

For an optimal path of cost \(C^*\), before termination there is a frontier
vertex reached with its optimal prefix cost. Its key is at most \(C^*\).
A removed goal has key equal to its route cost, so a goal costing more than
\(C^*\) cannot precede that frontier. This proves the stopping rule in exact
arithmetic. Discovery alone gives no such comparison.

The API name is retained for compatibility. Its queue rule is the A* method of
Hart, Nilsson and Raphael (1968), extended to a goal set. Berliner's 1979 paper,
*The B* tree search algorithm: A best-first proof procedure*, instead separates
optimistic and pessimistic game-tree bounds. This report studies the actual
datamunge graph API.

> Simple example: two possible destinations

Vertex 0 has a route to goal 2 costing \(1+5=6\), and a direct route to goal 3
costing 2. The lower bounds \((2,5,0,0)\) are exact. After removing 0, the queue
contains vertex 1 with key 6 and goal 3 with key 2; removing 3 proves the answer.

```{cpp, file="figures/b_star_goals.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::Diagram diagram;
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle style;
  style.width = 2.4;
  diagram.node("s", {0,0}, "0: start\nh = 2", style)
         .node("a", {5,2}, "1\nh = 5", style)
         .node("b", {10,2}, "2: goal\ncost = 6", style)
         .node("c", {5,-2}, "3: goal\ncost = 2", style)
         .edge("s","a","1").edge("a","b","5").edge("s","c","2")
         .text({5,-4}, "Choose the cheapest removed goal.");
  diagram.save_svg("build/b_star_goals.svg");
}
```
// result_begin:
// \image(figures/b_star_goals.svg)
// result_end
\caption(A hand-checkable directed graph. Both destinations are allowed, and only the route through vertex 3 costs 2.)
\alttext(Start 0 points to vertex 1 with weight 1 and goal 3 with weight 2. Vertex 1 points to goal 2 with weight 5.)

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto trace = dm::algorithms::b_star_search_trace(
      4, {{0,1,1},{1,2,5},{0,3,2}}, 0, {2,3}, {2,5,0,0});
  std::print("| removed vertex | g | g+h | entries left |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (const auto& step : trace.expansions)
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", step.vertex, step.path_cost, step.priority, step.queued);
  std::print("\nPath: {}; cost: {}; expanded: {}.\n",
             trace.result.path, trace.result.cost, trace.result.expanded);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| removed vertex | g | g+h | entries left |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0 | 0 | 2 | 0 |
| 3 | 2 | 2 | 1 |

Path: [0, 3]; cost: 2; expanded: 2.
// result_end
\caption(Actual non-stale queue removals. The expansion count includes the returned goal; queued entries may include obsolete labels.)

> Reopening when the bound is inconsistent

A bound is consistent when \(h(u)\le w(u,v)+h(v)\) on every edge. Consistency
means queue keys cannot decrease along an improving route and each vertex needs
at most one expansion. Admissibility alone still permits an estimate to fall
sharply across an edge; reopening then preserves the optimal-path frontier used
in the proof.

Consider edges \(0\to1\) costing 3, \(0\to2\) costing 1,
\(2\to1\) costing 1, and \(1\to3\) costing 3. Goal 4 is isolated.
The bound \((0,0,4,0,0)\) is admissible but inconsistent on \(2\to1\):
\(4>1+0\). Vertex 1 first expands with cost 3, then again with cost 2.
Its improved route reaches goal 3 at cost \(1+1+3=5\).

```{cpp, file="figures/b_star_reopen.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::Diagram diagram;
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle style;
  style.width = 2.3;
  diagram.node("s", {0,0}, "0: start", style)
         .node("a", {6,2}, "1: reopen\ng: 3 then 2", style)
         .node("b", {3,-2}, "2\nh = 4", style)
         .node("t", {11,2}, "3: goal\ncost = 5", style)
         .node("u", {11,-2}, "4: isolated\ngoal", style)
         .edge("s","a","3").edge("s","b","1")
         .edge("b","a","1").edge("a","t","3");
  diagram.save_svg("build/b_star_reopen.svg");
}
```
// result_begin:
// \image(figures/b_star_reopen.svg)
// result_end
\caption(A later improvement to vertex 1 must propagate to goal 3. An unreachable extra goal does not prevent finding a reachable one.)
\alttext(A direct edge to vertex 1 costs 3; reaching it through vertex 2 costs only 2. Vertex 1 then leads to goal 3 at additional cost 3.)

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto trace = dm::algorithms::b_star_search_trace(
      5, {{0,1,3},{0,2,1},{2,1,1},{1,3,3}}, 0, {3,4}, {0,0,4,0,0});
  std::print("| vertex | g | g+h |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (const auto& step : trace.expansions)
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} |\n", step.vertex, step.path_cost, step.priority);
  std::print("\nPath: {}; cost: {}.\n", trace.result.path, trace.result.cost);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| vertex | g | g+h |
| ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0 | 0 | 0 |
| 1 | 3 | 3 |
| 2 | 1 | 5 |
| 1 | 2 | 2 |
| 3 | 5 | 5 |

Path: [0, 2, 1, 3]; cost: 5.
// result_end
\caption(Vertex 1 is counted twice. The key decreases after vertex 2, as an inconsistent estimate allows.)

> Checking the theory and its limits

Keeping the same graph, increase only \(h(2)\). The exact remaining cost there
is 4. Values through 4 are admissible; sufficiently larger values delay vertex 2
until after the cost-6 goal is removed. The library checks dimensions and finite
values, but cannot establish admissibility without solving the path problem.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::print("| h(2) | admissible | cost | expansions |\n| ---: | :--- | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (double h : {0.,1.,2.,3.,4.,5.,6.,10.}) {
    const auto r = dm::algorithms::b_star_search(
        5, {{0,1,3},{0,2,1},{2,1,1},{1,3,3}}, 0, {3,4}, {0,0,h,0,0});
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", h, h <= 4, r.cost, r.expanded);
  }
  const auto missing = dm::algorithms::b_star_search(2, {}, 0, {1}, {0,0});
  const auto already = dm::algorithms::b_star_search(1, {}, 0, {0,0}, {0});
  std::print("\nUnreachable: found={}, path={}. Start is a goal: path={}, cost={}.\n",
             missing.found, missing.path, already.path, already.cost);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| h(2) | admissible | cost | expansions |
| ---: | :--- | ---: | ---: |
| 0 | true | 5 | 4 |
| 1 | true | 5 | 4 |
| 2 | true | 5 | 5 |
| 3 | true | 5 | 5 |
| 4 | true | 5 | 5 |
| 5 | false | 5 | 5 |
| 6 | false | 6 | 3 |
| 10 | false | 6 | 3 |

Unreachable: found=false, path=[]. Start is a goal: path=[0], cost=0.
// result_end
\caption(A controlled estimate sweep. An inadmissible estimate can still happen to succeed, so success at one value does not validate the bound.)

With \(n\) vertices and \(m\) edges, a consistent estimate gives at most one
non-stale removal per vertex and at most one successful relaxation per edge.
The lazy binary heap therefore costs \(O((n+m)\log(n+m))\) time and
\(O(n+m)\) storage. An inconsistent bound may produce exponentially many
re-expansions and queued improvements. The returned count measures queue removals,
including the goal, rather than distinct vertices or elapsed time.

No observed dataset is needed: these small directed graphs isolate the stopping
rule, disconnected goals, and reopening without introducing modelling assumptions.
All costs use double precision. Nonfinite weights or estimates are rejected;
overflowing accumulated costs or keys raise an error. Floating-point ties can
still affect which equally good path is returned.

> Conclusion

The four-vertex graph returns the cheaper goal after two removals. The second
graph demonstrates that admissible but inconsistent estimates can expand a vertex
twice while retaining the cheaper answer. Overestimation removes the guarantee.
Use zero bounds when a justified lower bound is unavailable; this is uniform-cost
search and remains optimal for nonnegative costs.

> References

- Hart, P. E., Nilsson, N. J. and Raphael, B. (1968). *A Formal Basis for the Heuristic Determination of Minimum Cost Paths*. IEEE Transactions on Systems Science and Cybernetics, 4(2), 100–107. https://doi.org/10.1109/TSSC.1968.300136
- Berliner, H. (1979). *The B* tree search algorithm: A best-first proof procedure*. Artificial Intelligence, 12(1), 23–40. https://doi.org/10.1016/0004-3702(79)90003-1
